# Tests

Tests run on **`bun:test`** and live next to what they cover (`src/core/mock/index.test.ts`).
The package suite is `bun run test`; the client suite under `test/` is `bun run test:clients`.

## Shape

- Import from `bun:test`: `import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'`. The oxlint `jest`
  plugin reads it.
- `it`, never `test` (`jest/consistent-test-it`). Titles describe the behavior in a sentence.
- A table is **`it.each` / `describe.each`**, never a loop around `it` (`jest/prefer-each`):
  - rows of values: `it.each([['a', 1], ['b', 2]] as const)('%s maps to %p', (name, n) => …)`;
  - rows of objects: `it.each([...cases])('$key does …', (testCase) => …)`. Spread a `readonly`
    table: bun's typing wants a mutable array for object rows;
  - a row that is itself an array is spread into arguments — wrap it: `inputs.map((i) => [i] as const)`;
  - a computed title is a precomputed `title` field, used as `'$title'`. Avoid `%#`, which bun
    formats inconsistently.
- `toStrictEqual` over `toEqual`; an equality matcher over `toBe(true)` on a comparison.
- `expect` only inside a test, never conditionally (`jest/no-conditional-expect`). For "this
  value, or anything when none is pinned" use `toStrictEqual(value ?? expect.any(String))`.
- `it.only`, `it.skip`, `xit`, `fit` and commented-out tests fail lint.
- A test that spawns a process (the CLI binary, `oxlint`) passes an explicit timeout as the
  third argument, as `src/index.test.ts` and `src/cli/watch.test.ts` do: a loaded machine takes
  longer than bun's 5s default.

## What tests may do that `src` may not

`*.test.ts` is exempt from the structural rules and the cast rules: `let`, `any`, type
assertions, arrow functions at module scope, empty stub callbacks, `console`, cross-layer
imports and awaiting inside a loop are all fine when they spell the fixture out most clearly.
Everything else in the TypeScript rule still applies.

## Working with the filesystem

- Generators resolve paths against `process.cwd()`. A test that writes files creates its own
  directory (`mkdtempSync`), `process.chdir`s into it, and restores the previous cwd in
  `afterEach` / `finally`. Name the prefix after the case in words (`asphodelos-per-kind-`).
- Remove temporary directories in `afterAll`. Work trees under the package use a `tmp-` prefix,
  which `.gitignore`, oxfmt and oxlint all skip.
- Run a generator Effect with `runGenerator` / `runGeneratorError` from `src/testing`.
- Background work (the Vite plugin) is awaited by polling for its observable result, never by a
  fixed sleep alone; wait until it has finished before the test ends, or its writes land in the
  next test's directory.

## Coverage of behavior

- A bug fix ships with a test that fails before it and passes after. When you add a guard, prove
  the test catches its absence (remove the guard, watch it fail, restore it).
- Assert through the observable result — files on disk, the generated source, a real request
  against the generated Elysia app (`src/core/runtime.test.ts`) — rather than internals.

## The client suite (`test/`)

- `cases/<name>/asphodelos.config.ts` + `tsconfig.json` per client library; `scripts/generate.ts`
  runs the **packaged** CLI over every case before each run, so `dist` must be current.
- `__generated__/` is output: gitignored, refreshed every run, never edited or committed.
- `runtime/` executes the generated hooks against `hosts/`; `types/` pins generics at compile
  time; `runtime/vite-plugin.test.ts` runs the packaged plugin inside a real Vite dev server.
- happy-dom is preloaded for React; a test that needs Node's timers (Vite) unregisters it for its
  duration and registers it again after.
- `bun -F @asphodelos/test typecheck` regenerates `__generated__` (through the packaged CLI, so
  after a build), compiles every case project, then the suite itself (`test/tsconfig.json`).
  `asphodelos` resolves to its source there through the `@asphodelos/source` condition, but the
  generated hooks the suite imports exist only after that generation step — which is why the
  suite's type-aware lint runs inside `test:clients`, after typecheck, and not in `bun run lint`.
- `bun run test:pack` (`scripts/pack-smoke.ts`) is the only check that installs the package the
  way users do, with npm and no lockfile. Run it after any change to `package.json`,
  `tsdown.config.ts`, the dependencies, or the entry points.
